1 NAME Johan Jakob Østenson /Vagle/
2 GIVN Johan Jakob Østenson
2 SURN Vagle
2 _MARNM Thorson
1 NAME John J. Thorson
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Litchville Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 STAE ND
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 26 MAR 1865
2 PLAC Vagle i Høyland
2 ADDR
3 CITY Sandnes
3 STAE Rogaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BAPM
2 DATE 16 APR 1865
2 ADDR
3 CITY Høyland
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 DEC 1929
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fargo
3 STAE ND
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Blacksmith/Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Reiste til Amerika, gift
2 CONT
2 CONT "JOHN THORSON
2 CONT
2 CONT John Thorson, resident of the Litchville community since 1902, died at the St. Luke's Hospital, Fargo, on Monday, December 16th, at four o'clock in the afternoon. Mrs. Thorson and all but two of the children were at his bedside wh
2 CONC en the end came, having been called to Fargo on Sunday. Deceased suffered an operation for a cancerous stomach tumor on December 9th since which time hope of his recovery had gradually declined. He was 64 years, 8 months and 20 da
2 CONC ys old when called by the angel of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born near Stavanger, Norway, on the 26th day of March, 1865, John Thorson remained in his native land until he was seventeen years of age coming to American in 1882. He came directly to what was then Dakota Territory. Being a blac
2 CONC ksmith by trade, he started a shop at Grand Rapids, LaMoure County, in 1883, where he remained for one year, following which he went to Valley City where he also engaged in that line of work. He remained in Valley City for a numbe
2 CONC r of years, during which time he was united in marriage to Miss Mathilda Oppegaard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after Litchville was started, in 1902, he came here, and for five years was an employee at the J. E. Warmbo blacksmith shop. In 1907 he purchased a farm in Litchville Township, the SE1/4 of Section 5, to which he moved wit
2 CONC h his family and where he has been at home ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are his widow and six children, namely: Carl, who has charge of the home farm; Mrs. Gabriel Berntson, of the north Hastings country; Mrs. Geo. F. Olson, of Tilford, S.D.; Mrs. Sam A. Anderson, Mrs. Richard Eilts and Lorr
2 CONC aine, all at home. One brother, Thore Wagle, remains on the old home place in Norway, and his four sisters are in this country. They are: Mrs. John Engberg of West Duluth; Mrs. Martin Peterson of Valley City and Mrs. Gabriel Bryne
2 CONC  of Kathryn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral will be held at the First Lutheran Church in this city this (Thursday) afternoon at two o'clock. Rev. J. S. Sunde, pastor of the church, will officiate, and interment will be made in the old Litchville cemetery, south 
2 CONC of town."
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in The Litchville Bulletin, Litchville, ND, December 1929. Bio by: Myrna (Lindstrom) McGregor
